  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow:\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon shows deep troughing centered \nalong and just west of the Pacific Coast, helping to prop up ridging \nacross the rest of the CONUS that is interrupted by a negatively-\ntilting shortwave trough pushing through the Central Plains. It's \nthat shortwave trough that is driving our dreary day so far, with \nlargely stratiform rainfall and drizzle wetting sidewalks that will \nbe buzzing with foot traffic later this evening. Light rain and \ndrizzle chances continue through the afternoon and evening, with any \ntotal amounts of rainfall expected to be very low (less than 0.1\"), \nallowing folks to be undeterred as long as an umbrella or rain coat \nare brought along. Short-term CAMs do depict a stray shower or two \naround sunset, staying limited in coverage by providing a slight \nchance for a rumble of thunder (5-10% chance). Temperatures before \nsunset will be in the mid-to-low 60s, and they should only slowly \ncool overnight as clouds insulate the surface. Temperatures only \ndrop into the 50s overnight, staying well above our normal for this \ntime of year (around 37 degrees).\n\nDreary and drizzly conditions are set to stick around tomorrow, with \nthe overall coverage of light rain and mist being lower than this \nafternoon/evening. An increased mass response in anticipation of the \ntrough to the west will help bump temperatures into the mid-to-upper \n60s, with warm air advection helping to create those light \nprecipitation chances. Later in the evening, rain chances increase \nas deep lift builds in from the southwest ahead the main trough. \nIncreasingly gusty winds also look to arrive, sending speeds into \nthe 25-35 mph range (for gusts) out of the south-southeast overnight \nheading into Monday.\n\nMonday and Beyond:\n\nBy Monday morning, the main story of the weather week arrive as a \nsurface low takes shape over northeast Colorado, backed up by the \ndeep longwave troughing that neutrally tilts before sending the main \nsurface low northeast through the forecast area. As it does so, rain \nand storm chances also move through, including potentially strong \nones across far southeast Nebraska into eastern Kansas where shear \nvalues are maximized along the southwest-to-northeast oriented \nPacific front. Farther north, instability quickly drops off but \nnot to zero, leaving lone rumbles of thunder possible to \ninterrupt the soothing rain sounds that will accompany the \nsystem's passing. It's at this point where things start to \ndiverge in terms of of the evolution of the ejecting system. \n\nIt's looking increasingly likely that despite being initially \ndry- slotted, the main upper low remains farther to the south \n(with the the ECMWF and AI-GFS), allowing the deformation band \nto stick around well into and potentially through Tuesday \ncompared to the GFS. Highs on the backside of this system will \ntake a hit back into the 50s (potentially upper 40s for \nnortheast Nebraska), and regardless of the evolution of the \nsystem, we'll be dealing with very gusty winds that top out in \nthe 35-45 mph range Tuesday into Wednesday out of the northwest.\nSimilar if not slightly warmer temperatures hang around for the\nremaining portion of the week, as largely zonal mid/upper flow \ntaking us into the weekend, interrupted by a compact shortwave \ntrough translating eastward Thursday into Friday. Some kind of \nlight rainfall seems likely with its passage by due to its \ncompact nature, those chances will remain low until better \nagreement aligns.",
